#!/usr/bin/python3
# vibeserver-pocket — the POCKET VIBESERVER's Wi-Fi, as root (2026-10-10).
#
# ★★★ WHAT THIS DOES. A pocket box (a Pi 3 A+ and friends, flashed with image/pocket) has ONE Wi-Fi
# radio and no screen. This service decides, all the time, what that radio is doing:
#
#     no saved network ............................ OPEN setup hotspot "VibeServer"  (first boot only)
#     saved networks, one in range ................ join it — network 1 before 2 before 3, ALWAYS
#     saved networks, none in range ............... the owner's SECURED fallback hotspot (WPA2)
#     on the fallback hotspot, nobody connected ... look again every RESCAN_SEC, rejoin when one returns
#     joined network 2 or 3, network 1 comes back . move up to network 1 (UPGRADE_SEC)
#
# The full design, the boot state machine and the on-device checklist: docs/POCKET-VIBESERVER-PI3A.md.
#
# ★★★ STRICT ORDER, NOT "STRONGEST" OR "LAST USED" (Stuart, 2026-10-10). NetworkManager is given
#     autoconnect-priority 30/20/10 so its OWN choice at boot already prefers 1, then 2, then 3 —
#     but NM never moves off a working connection to a better one, and this box must. So this
#     service does the joining itself, in order, and treats NM's autoconnect as the fast path only.
#
# ★★★ WHY ROOT, AND WHY A SEPARATE SERVICE. vibeserver (the daemon) runs unprivileged with
#     NoNewPrivileges, exactly as on every install. It WRITES A REQUEST into its own state directory
#     (/var/lib/vibeserver/pocket-wifi.request, pocket-kick.request) and this service reads it —
#     the same boundary as vibeserver-maintenance, one layer over. Not the maintenance helper
#     itself: that is a one-shot with a log the admin page tails, and this is a long-running state
#     machine that must own the radio between requests.
#  ★★ EVERY FIELD IS RE-VALIDATED HERE. The daemon validated too, but this is the side that runs as
#     root, so it trusts nothing it reads: a regular file, not a link, owned by the vibeserver user,
#     at most 16 KB, every SSID and password re-checked. nmcli is called with ARGUMENT LISTS, never
#     a shell, so an SSID like `"; reboot` is just an odd name.
#  ★★★ PASSWORDS ARE NEVER LOGGED. They exist in the request (0600, deleted the moment it is read),
#     in nmcli's argv for the instant it runs, and in NetworkManager's keyfiles
#     (/etc/NetworkManager/system-connections, root 0600). Nothing here prints an argument list.
#
# ★ OFF unless /etc/vibeserver-pocket/enabled exists (the unit's ConditionPathExists, and checked
#   again here): the package ships this file to every install and it does nothing on any of them.

